#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""bashd — disconnect-proof local & remote bash for agents.

Design: https://github.com/.../bashd + docs/RESEARCH.md (summary below).

- One file, Python stdlib only. Two faces: MCP stdio server (`bashd mcp`) and CLI.
- All state and execution live on the TARGET host (~/.bashd/jobs/<id>/ files or a
  target-side tmux session). The connection is a cache: losing it at any instant
  loses no work and no output; every call reconnects and resumes by byte cursor.
- Sync `run` = sugar over the job substrate (idempotent start + resumable tail);
  timeouts DETACH (never kill). Killing is an explicit `signal`.
- Transport is system ssh only (ControlMaster, ProxyJump paths, keepalives).
  Ids are client-generated so `start` is idempotent across dropped requests.
- Routes: every host may list several paths (direct / hop), optionally with a
  "cost"; calls try the sticky path first, then the rest cheapest-first, and
  a detached background probe re-latches the cheapest path when it recovers.
  A mid-execution transport death moves the whole control script to the next
  path (safe: scripts exec_script runs are idempotent by substrate design —
  user commands live target-side in tmux + files, never inside the ssh call).
- v0.4 `term` + wire: a HUMAN terminal on any persistent session — turn-based
  by nature (a local line editor, so only Enter/Ctrl-C cross the network) that
  behaves like an ordinary terminal over a good link (raw byte passthrough,
  output pushed at 10 Hz, resize forwarded). Rides a custom transport for
  extremely slow/unstable networks: one long-lived ssh channel running a tiny
  target-side frame agent (gzip-tagged payloads, reconnect-and-resume by byte
  cursor) instead of one ssh exec per operation. --no-wire falls back to the
  classic per-call transport with the same UX.

Substrate layout on a target host:
  ~/.bashd/jobs/<id>/run out err code lock session (batch jobs; the lock is
    held (flock) for the job's whole execution — the liveness marker)
  ~/.bashd/tmux/<name>.log                          (tty session pipe-pane log)
"""

